010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A $3\\times3$ matrix has rank $2$. Is it invertible?","options":["No, because it does not have full rank","Yes, because its rank is positive","Yes, because it is square","Only if its determinant equals $2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8786fdd0333d7206d526d15b","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch7_rank_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is the rank of $\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$2$","$1$","$0$","$4$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9bd9b930628f721019b6148b","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch7_rank_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$, what is $\\operatorname{rank}(A)+\\dim(\\ker A)$?","options":["$n$, the number of columns","$m$, the number of rows","$mn$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"542cba0edf3b2e887db47da8","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch7_rank_011_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}$ be a $3\\times5$ matrix with $\\operatorname{rank}(\\boldsymbol{A})=2$. What is the range of the linear map $\\boldsymbol{x}\\mapsto\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{x}$?","options":["A $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^5$","All of $\\mathbb{R}^3$","A $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$","A $3$-dimensional subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^5$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"da32486e8bfef6e9573b9817","time_limit":35},{"id":"la_batch7_rank_017_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Different sequences of row operations can be used to reduce the same matrix. Why does this not make its rank ambiguous?","options":["Every sequence ends in the same RREF, so the number of nonzero rows is the same.","Every valid sequence must use the same row operations in the same order.","Row operations never turn a nonzero row in